# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ── 4. Watchlist: label collisions that are NOT identity (do not align) ────────
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The census surfaced these same-label clusters that must NOT receive
# owl:equivalentClass/equivalentProperty — the shared label masks genuinely
# different terms. Recorded so a future census pass doesn't 'fix' them:
#
#   - 'status'/'name'/'email'/'customerId': the truck-ontology-bench properties
#     (truckStatus, driverStatus, loadStatus, customerName, driverEmail, ...)
#     are domain-scoped and disjoint from the semantic-medallion generic
#     properties sharing the label fragment. Different domains, different
#     semantics — alignment would corrupt inference.
#   - 'Customer': moderndata101#Customer (data-product consumer) vs
#     truck-ontology#Customer (freight customer) — same label, different
#     ontological commitments. rdfs:seeAlso at most, never equivalence.
#   - 'Concept' / 'Technology': too generic to assert identity across
#     documents without inspecting instance usage; leave unaligned.

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ── 5. External well-known-ontology alignments (distinct from Sections 1-3) ────
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Sections 1-3 repair disjoint IRIs re-minted for the SAME concept across this
# corpus. This section is different in kind: it cross-references a locally-minted
# term to a genuinely EXTERNAL, independently-published ontology for interop with
# tools/reasoners outside this corpus — not a duplicate-repair.

:p-plan-hospital-os-alignment a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:name "P-Plan alignment: hospital-os-rdf-harness workflow/sequencing terms"@en ;
    schema:description "Cross-referenced 2026-08-04 in https://linkeddata.uriburner.com/DAV/demos/daas/hospital-os-rdf-harness/ontology.ttl per user request, evaluating whether the doc's Workflow-related TBox terms should align to a shared/standard vocabulary. schema.org has no formal Workflow/Step class (schema:HowTo/HowToStep serves instructional content, a different purpose than process modeling, and is used separately in the same document for :sepsisPathwayHowTo). P-Plan (http://purl.org/net/p-plan#), a W3C-community ontology for plans composed of steps, was judged the better fit."@en ;
    schema:about <https://linkeddata.uriburner.com/DAV/demos/daas/hospital-os-rdf-harness/ontology.ttl#hospitalOS> ;
    rdfs:comment """Alignment triples (in the source document, not duplicated here):
  os:ClinicalPathway  owl:equivalentClass  p-plan:Plan .
  os:WorkflowStep     owl:equivalentClass  p-plan:Step .
  os:hasStep          owl:inverseOf        p-plan:isStepOfPlan .   # direction-reversed: os:hasStep(Pathway,Step) vs p-plan:isStepOfPlan(Step,Plan)
  os:nextStep         owl:inverseOf        p-plan:isPrecededBy .   # direction-reversed: os:nextStep(A,B)="B follows A" vs p-plan:isPrecededBy(B,A)="B preceded by A"

Property alignments use owl:inverseOf rather than owl:equivalentProperty because P-Plan's argument order is reversed relative to this ontology's — equivalentProperty would assert a false direction-preserving equivalence. Tradeoff noted to the user before implementing: P-Plan is a niche vocabulary (mostly scientific-workflow/provenance contexts) — real interop value only if a consumer actually has a P-Plan-aware tool/reasoner; otherwise it's a fourth external prefix with no practical payoff. User approved proceeding."""@en ;
    schema:dateCreated "2026-08-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ── 6. Intelligence Allocation Ontology (intel:) — 2026-08-19 ─────────────────
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Minted by the Right-Sizing Your Intelligence Spend collection (DeepSeek V4 Flash,
# 2026-08-19) because no shared ontology covers workload intelligence thresholds /
# tier routing. Registered here per cross-document-local-term-reuse.ttl so future
# collections bind the DAV namespace below instead of re-minting disjoint terms.
#   intel: = <https://linkeddata.uriburner.com/DAV/demos/daas/ontology/intelligence-allocation#>
# Classes: Workload, Workflow, WorkloadProfile, IntelligenceThreshold, IntelligenceTier,
#   ValueClass, BottleneckType, ExecutionSurface, RoutingPolicy
# Properties: hasIntelligenceThreshold, requiresTier, hasValueClass, hasBottleneck,
#   belongsToWorkflow (rdfs:subPropertyOf schema:isPartOf), routesTo, appliesTo,
#   verifiesVia, hasTokenSensitivity, hasOverqualificationRisk,
#   hasMarginalValueOfIntelligence, intelligenceConsumedPerOutcome, hasWorkflowDomain

:intelIntelligenceAllocation a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Intelligence Allocation Ontology (intel:)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Canonical DAV namespace for the Intelligence Allocation Ontology minted 2026-08-19 in right-sizing-intelligence-spend-deepseek_v4flash-1.ttl (1,483-triple collection). Nine classes and thirteen properties, every term carrying rdfs:label/comment/domain/range and verified external cross-references (dbpedia:Business_process, dbpedia:Artificial_intelligence, dbpedia:Basic_research, dbpedia:Applied_science, dbpedia:Automation, dbpedia:Software_agent, dbpedia:Decision-making, dbpedia:Enterprise_software, wd:Q11538, wd:Q1418791, wd:Q3510521)."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy :ontology ;
    schema:dateCreated "2026-08-19T14:43:35Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://linkeddata.uriburner.com/DAV/demos/daas/ontology/intelligence-allocation#> .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ── 7. LLM Routing Ontology (llmr:) — 2026-08-19 ──────────────────────────────
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Minted by the llm-routing-skill (DeepSeek V4 Flash, 2026-08-19) to type the
# capability x cost x latency routing graph. Registered here per
# cross-document-local-term-reuse.ttl so future collections bind the namespace
# below instead of re-minting disjoint terms.
#   llmr: = <https://www.openlinksw.com/ontology/llm-routing#>
# Classes: Model, TaskType, CapabilityLevel, GovernancePolicy, FeedbackRecord
# Properties: modelId, vendor, inputPricePerMTok, outputPricePerMTok,
#   cachedInputPricePerMTok, costTier, latencyClass, contextWindowTokens,
#   priceUpdated, graphPriceUpdated, paretoFrontierModel, dominantDimension,
#   profileNote, tokenSensitivity, overqualificationRisk, requiredCapabilityLevel,
#   approvesModel, blocksModel, residencyConstraint, feedbackModel, feedbackTask,
#   qualityScore, passedVerification, observedLatency, feedbackDate
# schema.org terms used by the graph: schema:additionalProperty +
#   schema:PropertyValue (capability attributes), schema:ItemList +
#   schema:ListItem + schema:position (escalation ladders).
# Sets are repeated triples, not RDF lists (migrated 2026-08-19 from
#   RDF-collection encoding of paretoFrontier/escalationLadder/capabilityProfile
#   after the list form forced rdf:rest*/rdf:first traversal in every SPARQL
#   query and silently truncated frontiers at 12 members).
# NO BLANK NODES (preferences.ttl Step 37): reification nodes (schema:ListItem,
#   schema:PropertyValue) carry named hash IRIs (full <IRI> syntax in Turtle —
#   '#' is not legal in a prefixed-name local part); priceUpdated/graphPriceUpdated
#   split into single-domain properties rather than a blank-node owl:unionOf.
# TERM DEFINITIONS: authoritative TBox is
#   llm-routing-skill/references/llm-routing-ontology.ttl, embedded verbatim into
#   routing-graph.ttl by build_routing_graph.py; every term carries
#   rdfs:label/comment/domain/range/isDefinedBy + verified external
#   cross-references per the ontology-cross-reference gate (2026-08-19).
# Model entities: llmrm: <https://www.openlinksw.com/ontology/llm-routing/models/>
# Task entities:  llmrt: <https://www.openlinksw.com/ontology/llm-routing/tasks/>
# Vendor entities: llmrv: <https://www.openlinksw.com/ontology/llm-routing/vendors/>
# Level entities: llmrl: <https://www.openlinksw.com/ontology/llm-routing/levels/>

:llmrLlmRouting a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "LLM Routing Ontology (llmr:)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Canonical namespace for the LLM Routing Ontology minted 2026-08-19 in llm-routing-skill/references/routing-graph.ttl (7,547-triple graph, 145 models, 30 task types). Model registry + capability x cost x latency profiles + per-task Pareto frontiers and escalation ladders, informing intelligent LLM routing. Term definitions live in the authoritative TBox llm-routing-skill/references/llm-routing-ontology.ttl (embedded into the graph; all 31 used terms carry label/comment/domain/range/isDefinedBy + verified cross-refs per the ontology-cross-reference gate). Zero blank nodes (Step 37): reification nodes are named hash IRIs. rdfs:seeAlso the intel: Intelligence Allocation Ontology for tier/execution-surface semantics."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy :ontology ;
    schema:dateCreated "2026-08-19T15:10:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.openlinksw.com/ontology/llm-routing#> ,
        <https://linkeddata.uriburner.com/DAV/demos/daas/ontology/intelligence-allocation#> .

# ── Agentic Commerce Stack Mesh Properties (minted 2026-08-20 in
#    openrouter-stripe-intelligence-network-meshup-deepseek_v4flash-1.ttl) ──
# Five owl:ObjectProperty terms minted document-locally in the a16z
# intelligence-network meshup (:meshupOntology, @prefix : =
# https://www.a16z.news/p/openrouter-and-stripe-the-intelligence#).
# First corpus registration (the prior open-agentic-economy session minted
# implementsProtocol / isReferenceImplementationOf document-locally without
# registering). Future documents needing these concepts should REUSE the
# document-local IRIs via owl:equivalentProperty, not re-mint.

:meshPropertyImplementsProtocol a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "implements protocol"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a software application or service to the protocol it implements (e.g. x402-buyer -> x402). Document-local canonical: <https://www.a16z.news/p/openrouter-and-stripe-the-intelligence#implementsProtocol> and <https://www.circle.com/blog/building-the-open-agentic-economy#implementsProtocol>."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy :ontology .

:meshPropertyIsReferenceImplementationOf a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "is reference implementation of"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a concrete, executable implementation to the protocol or standard it demonstrates (e.g. x402-buyer -> x402). Document-local canonical: <https://www.a16z.news/p/openrouter-and-stripe-the-intelligence#isReferenceImplementationOf>."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy :ontology .

:meshPropertyRoutesIntelligence a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "routes intelligence"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a software application or service to the intelligence-network function of routing model, token, and information flows between businesses (OpenRouter, Ori Eval, llm-routing-skill). Document-local canonical: <https://www.a16z.news/p/openrouter-and-stripe-the-intelligence#routesIntelligence>."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy :ontology .

:meshPropertySettlesValue a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "settles value"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a software application, service, or protocol to the settlement of value - dollars, tokens, or other media of exchange (Stripe, x402, x402 Facilitator). Document-local canonical: <https://www.a16z.news/p/openrouter-and-stripe-the-intelligence#settlesValue>."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy :ontology .

:meshPropertyRepresentsCommerce a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "represents commerce"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a document or framework to the representation of commerce as a hyperlink-based entity-relationship graph of principals, agents, offers, licenses, and access rights (LOAC). Document-local canonical: <https://www.a16z.news/p/openrouter-and-stripe-the-intelligence#representsCommerce>."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy :ontology .

# ── ArchitecturalTier — 2 disjoint re-mints aligned (2026-08-21) ──

:ArchitecturalTier a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Architectural Tier"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A functional layer within a conceptual data virtualization architecture (e.g. physical relational storage, conceptual mapping/virtualization, semantic graph and inference, universal consumption). Canonical corpus IRI; 2 disjoint re-mints aligned: Gemini 3.7 Flash collection (2026-08-21) and DeepSeek V4 Flash collection (2026-08-21-2), both minting the same document-local IRI under the Medium post URL. Future documents needing this concept should REUSE this IRI, not re-mint."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy :ontology ;
    owl:equivalentClass <https://medium.com/virtuoso-blog/conceptual-data-virtualization-for-sql-and-rdf-using-open-standards-24520925c7ce#ArchitecturalTier> .

# ── IntegrationApproach — 2 disjoint re-mints aligned (2026-08-21) ──

:IntegrationApproach a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Integration Approach"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A methodology for combining or accessing multi-source relational and graph data (e.g. SQL-only RDBMS worldview, dedicated graph store 'NoSQL' worldview, multi-model conceptual virtualization). Canonical corpus IRI; 2 disjoint re-mints aligned: Gemini 3.7 Flash collection (2026-08-21) and DeepSeek V4 Flash collection (2026-08-21-2), both minting the same document-local IRI under the Medium post URL. Future documents needing this concept should REUSE this IRI, not re-mint."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy :ontology ;
    owl:equivalentClass <https://medium.com/virtuoso-blog/conceptual-data-virtualization-for-sql-and-rdf-using-open-standards-24520925c7ce#IntegrationApproach> .
